"text": "If you plan to shop your Medicare coverage this fall, watch this first before making a very costly mistake. Every fall, I talk to folks who switch plans to save forty dollars a month, and it costs them thousands. Worse yet, many realize too late that they could never get their original Medicare coverage back. I am Eric. I have been in the insurance business since 1994, helping people navigate Medicare with confidence. 2027 is a massive year for Medicare changes. Supplement rates are climbing, and millions face plan cancellations. Plus, many standalone drug plans will cost much more next year because temporary federal subsidies are ending December 31st. Today, I will walk you through how to safely shop your Medicare Supplement, Medicare Advantage, and Part D plans. Stick around until the end, where I share our rate increase hack to lower your supplement costs without sacrificing protection. Let us start with the coverage where one wrong move is hardest to undo, your Medicare Supplement or Medigap plan. The biggest misconception is thinking the fall annual enrollment period lets you freely switch Medigap plans without health questions. From October 15th to December 7th, anyone can change their Medicare Advantage or Part D prescription drug plan. However, that fall window gives you zero special rights to change your Medicare Supplement policy without medical underwriting. Everyone gets one six-month Medigap open enrollment window when they first turn sixty-five and enroll in Medicare Part B. Once that six-month window closes, switching Medigap plans or carriers in most states requires passing health underwriting questions. That means an insurance company can legally review your health history, charge you higher rates, or decline your application completely. Now, several states do offer special birthday rules or anniversary rules that let existing policyholders switch without health questions. However, state rules vary wildly. In states like Kentucky or West Virginia, you can only switch within your current carrier. Irony is states with birthday rules are seeing the steepest rate hikes because carriers must accept higher-risk applicants guaranteed issue. Years ago, Medigap rate increases averaged five to eight percent, and premiums over two hundred and fifty dollars were rare. Today, standard Plan G rates are climbing rapidly, pushing many beneficiaries to consider Plan N or High-Deductible Plan G. Plan N is a strong alternative to Plan G. You still only pay the standard annual Medicare Part B deductible. The trade-off with Plan N is paying up to twenty dollars for doctor visits and fifty dollars for emergency rooms. Plan N also does not cover Part B excess charges, though very few doctors nationwide actually bill for those today. Meanwhile, High-Deductible Plan G is surging in popularity. For 2027, the annual deductible is three thousand and fifty dollars. You do not pay full medical prices toward that deductible. You only pay the twenty percent Medicare coinsurance share. Think of that three thousand and fifty dollars as an annual out-of-pocket maximum before the plan pays one hundred percent. If you have chronic conditions or see specialists frequently, staying on traditional Plan G may still be your best value. Remember, if you downgrade from Plan G, getting your old Plan G back later will likely require passing medical underwriting. When shopping Medigap, remember that policies do not reset January 1st. Rates typically adjust on your policy anniversary month. Also, Medigap benefits are standardized by law. A Plan G or Plan N offers identical medical coverage across every carrier. However, underwriting questions vary by company. One carrier might decline a health condition, while another carrier approves you immediately. Golden rule: Never cancel your current Medigap policy until your new policy is officially approved and in your hands. Also, getting approved for a new Medigap policy does not automatically cancel your old one. You must call to cancel. We have seen folks accidentally pay for two Medigap plans for months because they assume the switch happened automatically. Finally, you can shop Medigap three hundred and sixty-five days a year. Waiting until after December 7th often means faster processing.",
